# `parallel:` is the only concurrency in NX and no benchmark touches it.
#
# The rule that shapes this workload is in grammar section 2.3: a
# `parallel:` task may not write a name local to the enclosing function,
# and tasks that conflict are serialised in program order. So the tasks here
# write two distinct module-level names, which is the only shape that is
# actually parallel, and the reference is the same work run sequentially.
#
# `burn` is a pure Int loop, so NX will also memoize it -- but its argument
# is the same two constants every time, which means one memo entry per
# distinct argument and no thrash. What this row measures is the thread
# pool: whether two conflict-free tasks really do run at the same time.
#
# A conflict is the control: the same two tasks writing the same name.

burn = 150000000

fn burn_it(k):
    t = 0
    i = 0
    while i < burn:
        t = t + (i * k) % 1009
        i = i + 1
    return t

a = 0
b = 0
parallel:
    a = burn_it(1)
    b = burn_it(2)
print(a, b)

# The control: the same two tasks, both writing `a`, so they serialise.
c = 0
parallel:
    c = burn_it(3)
    c = burn_it(4)
print(c)